  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n- Clearing tonight, dry through Wednesday\n\nThe upper low that created showers yesterday is exiting the state\nto the east. Visible satellite loop shows a well defined back edge\nto the clouds and clearing has reached the lake shore. As the\nupper low moves east and ridging builds in from the west, the\nclearing will move across the rest of the cwa this\nafternoon/evening.\n\nThe upper flow pattern across the northern tier of states is\ngenerally northwest to southeast. Typical of this time of year,\nshort waves moving through the flow will cross the state\nfrequently. The next wave will move through Sunday followed by\nanother wave over northern Lower Tuesday. A much more robust short\nwave will dig south across the Great Lakes Wednesday night and\nThursday. The wave that moves through Sunday has little moisture\nwith it and we won't see anything more than clouds late tonight \nand Sunday morning.\n\nThe short wave Tuesday moves across Lake Superior and won't\nproduce much more than some clouds Tuesday night and Wednesday. \n\nThe deeper wave Wednesday/Thursday may give us some precipitation,\ndepending on where the surface low ends up. Models are showing the\nsurface low moving across the northern OH Valley \nWednesday/Thursday and would pull the precipitation south. The \nexception may be for the lake shore area with persistent north \nflow and colder temperatures may produce some lake effect rain \nshowers. For now, we'll \n\n- Warmer than normal temperatures through mid week\n\nHighs are expected in the mid 50s to lower 60s through Wednesday,\nbefore cooling into the 40s Thursday/Friday. Normal highs are\naround 50.",
